  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This lace beret is worked bottom-up in the round. It starts from a fitted brim band, increases to a wide disc carrying an all-over feather-and-fan lace pattern, then decreases to a gathered centre. Fits adult heads of 54 (56, 58) cm."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Bottom-up construction is chosen because the lace pattern is worked on the disc section only, after the brim band establishes the head circumference. The feather-and-fan repeat uses "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "yarn-over"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "yarn over"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " increases balanced by k2tog and ssk decreases on the same round, so the stitch count stays constant across the lace section. The "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "magic-loop"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "magic loop"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " method handles both the narrow band and the wide disc on one needle. About 100 g (220 m) of DK wool. Block aggressively over a dinner plate; lace only reveals itself after blocking."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What you need"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "suppliesCard",
        "attrs": {
          "heading": "Materials",
          "items": [
            {
              "name": "DK-weight wool yarn (wool blocks best for lace)",
              "qty": "100 g (approx. 220 m)",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "4 mm circular needle, 80 cm (UK 8 / US 6)",
              "qty": "1",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "3.5 mm circular needle, 80 cm (UK 9 / US 4) -- for brim band",
              "qty": "1",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Stitch markers",
              "qty": "9 (1 BOR + 8 sections)",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Dinner plate (approx. 28 cm) -- for blocking",
              "qty": "1",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Blocking pins",
              "qty": "12+",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Tapestry needle and scissors",
              "qty": "1 each",
              "substitutions": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Gauge"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "22 sts x 30 rounds = 10 x 10 cm in stocking stitch on 4 mm needles, blocked. For the lace disc, gauge is approximate: feather-and-fan opens significantly in blocking. Swatch the lace pattern and block the swatch before measuring. If the lace is tighter than 20 sts per 10 cm after blocking, go up a needle size."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Stitches used"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "k -- knit; p -- purl"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "yo -- yarn over (creates an eyelet hole, adds 1 stitch)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "k2tog -- knit 2 together, right-leaning decrease"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "ssk -- slip, slip, knit, left-leaning decrease"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "m1r -- make one right (for disc shaping increases, not lace)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pattern"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Before you cast on, circle your size -- every count reads S (M, L)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Cast on"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Using "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "magic-loop"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "magic loop"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " on the 3.5 mm needle, cast on 116 (120, 128) sts using long-tail cast-on. Tail formula: tail_cm = (needle mm x stitch count) / 10 + 15. For size L on a 3.5 mm needle: (3.5 x 128) / 10 + 15 = 60 cm; round up to 65 cm. Join without twisting, place BOR marker."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Brim band"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Work 8 rounds of k1 p1 rib on 3.5 mm needle. Switch to 4 mm needle. After the brim, you should have 116 (120, 128) stitches on the needle."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Increase section"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Place 8 markers. Increase round: *k to marker, "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "make-one-lace"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "m1r"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", slip marker; repeat from * 8 times. Work plain round. Repeat these 2 rounds until approx. 192 (200, 208) sts. After the increase section, you should have approximately 192 (200, 208) stitches on the needle."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Lace disc"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Feather-and-fan round (work on 18-st repeat): *k2tog x3, [yo k1] x6, ssk x3; repeat from * to end. Plain round: knit all. Repeat these 2 rounds 6 times across the disc. The lace and the shaping increases are worked separately: complete all shaping increases first, then work the lace pattern on the established disc."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Crown decreases"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Transition round: knit all. Maintain 8 decrease markers."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Decrease round 1: *k to 2 sts before marker, k2tog; repeat from * 8 times. 184 (192, 200) sts."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Round 2: knit all."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Continue alternating until 32 sts remain, then decrease every round until 8 sts."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "After the final decrease round, you should have 8 stitches on the needle, ready to gather."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Top finish"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Cut yarn with 20 cm tail. Draw through all 8 sts, pull firmly, fasten on inside."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Common faults"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Lace pattern looks like stockinette: lace only opens in blocking. The pattern is correct if the yarn-overs and decreases are placed correctly; do not attempt to judge lace from the needles."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Stitch count drifts on lace rounds: the yarn-overs and decreases must balance. Every 18-stitch feather-and-fan repeat starts with 18 sts and ends with 18 sts. Count after each lace round."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Disc does not open to 28 cm after blocking: insufficient blocking. Soak until saturated, stretch aggressively over the plate, pin every 2 cm around the brim band, and allow to dry bone dry before unpinning."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Finishing"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Weave in ends. Soak for 20 minutes. Stretch over a dinner plate, pin every 2 cm around the brim band, and dry completely before removing."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Care"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Hand wash cool in wool wash. Re-block over the plate after each wash to restore the disc shape and open the lace."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What to try next"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The summer lace hat applies the same feather-and-fan pattern to a straight cylindrical crown rather than a wide disc -- a hat that functions more like a beanie."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}